            <div xml:id="d139">

                <pb facs="0300" n="263" sameAs="frus:frus1969-76v05/pg263"/>

                <head>139. Telegram From the Embassy in France to the
                        Department of State<anchor n="1" xml:id="d139-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Paris</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1972-10-18T16:52:00+00:00">October 18, 1972,
                        1652Z</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>19905. Subject: SC Meeting in Panama. Ref: Paris
                        19729.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d139-f2"/>
                </p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>In conversation October 18 on unrelated subjects, Quai Director American
                        Affairs told PolCouns that instructions on possible SC
                        <pb facs="0301" n="264" xml:id="pg264"/> meeting in Panama were sent to
                        French Embassy Washington on October 17.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Essence of message to French Embassy is: <list>
                            <label>A)</label>
                            <item>
                                Schumann feels he cannot reneg
                                on his commitment to Latin Americans to support Latin American
                                initiative to hold SC meeting in
                                Panama. (French working level had not briefed Schumann on possible SC meeting in Panama prior to his
                                September dinner with Latin Americans since “idea had been
                                germinating for about eight months and US reps had not made a
                                particular issue of it.”)</item>
                            <label>B)</label>
                            <item>Ever since Ambassador’s representation to Schumann on October 5 (Paris
                                    18969),<anchor n="3" xml:id="d139-f3"/> French reps in New York
                                and elsewhere have been under strict instructions not to
                                “proselytize” on behalf of Panamanian proposal. French will continue
                                to maintain this “discreet” position.</item>
                        </list>
                    </item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>PolCouns commented that while he could appreciate FonMin’s’ difficulty in reneging on his
                        commitment to Latin Americans, something more than apparent neutrality of
                        French position was needed in the circumstances. Speaking personally,
                        PolCouns asked Quai Director whether he could consider instructing French
                        reps, without reneging on Schumann’s
                        promise, to attempt persuade Latin Americans of wisdom in the circumstances
                        of not pressing issue to a vote. Without committing himself, Quai Director
                        said he would look into this possibility.</item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#bold">Watson</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
